{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1151 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5851983bb2405b8aea0a99afb37adaa01464ce31f9000bf68fb62df501f7b034" :edb/db-before-t 1150 :edb/db-after-t 1151 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534463 50 #inst "2026-10-07T06:13:18.283Z" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078230 1100 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,$?\n${3,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 2x + 3,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0$" 13194139534463 false] [17592186078230 1100 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078230 1145 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-2}^{5}f(x)dx &= ∫_{-2}^{0}3dx + ∫_{0}^{5}(2x + 3)dx \\\\\n&= 3x \\mid _{-2}^{0} + (x^{2} + 3x) \\mid _{0}^{5} \\\\\n&= [3(0) - 3(-2)] + [(5^{2} + 3(5)) - (0^{2} + 3(0))] \\\\\n&= 6 + 25 + 15 \\\\\n&= 46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 false] [17592186078230 1145 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x{|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =[3(0)-3(-2)]+[({5}^{2}+3(5))-({0}^{2}+3(0))] \\\\  & =6+25+15 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078231 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078231 1100 "The graph of $y=f(x)$, shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/e969825468755643c99584897eed6f04.png)" 13194139534463 false] [17592186078231 1145 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $[MATH: x \\in [0,2)]$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4]$. Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $[MATH: x \\in [0,2).]$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{1}|= bh; = 2 \\cdot 2; = 4$.\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4]$. The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2}bh; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2; = 2$.\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n$\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2$.\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{4}f(x)dx &= A_{1} - A_{2} \\\\\n&= 4 - 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 false] [17592186078231 1145 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [0,2)$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078232 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078232 1100 "Given the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/eb1ab74ae544a1b33f0f6c89141309e6.png)" 13194139534463 false] [17592186078232 1145 "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0]$, a line segment for $x\\in (0,2)$, a line segment for $[MATH: x\\in (2,4],]$ and the point at $x=2$. So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-4}^{4}f(x)dx &= ∫_{-4}^{0}f(x)dx + ∫_{0}^{2}f(x)dx + ∫_{2}^{2}f(x)dx + ∫_{2}^{4}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-4}^{0}f(x)dx_(⏟)_(A_{1}) + ∫_{0}^{2}f(x)dx_(⏟)_(-A_{2}) + 0 + ∫_{2}^{4}f(x)dx_(⏟)_(A_{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0]$. The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2}πr^{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot π \\cdot 2^{2}; = 2π$.\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2)$. The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2}bh; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2; = 2$.\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n$\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2$.\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $[MATH: x \\in (2,4].]$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n$A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2}bh; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2; = 2$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-4}^{4}f(x)dx &= A_{1} - A_{2} + A_{3} \\\\\n&= 2π - 2 + 2 \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 false] [17592186078232 1145 "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078516 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289612 13194139534463 true] [17592186078516 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289633 13194139534463 true] [17592186078516 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289640 13194139534463 true] [17592186078516 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289657 13194139534463 true] [17592186078517 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289619 13194139534463 true] [17592186078517 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289647 13194139534463 true] [17592186078517 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289650 13194139534463 true] [17592186078517 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289667 13194139534463 true] [17592186078518 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289626 13194139534463 true] [17592186078518 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289654 13194139534463 true] [17592186078518 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289660 13194139534463 true] [17592186078518 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289674 13194139534463 true] [17592186078661 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534463 true] [17592186078661 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/824f039d5f015a2cd2165cfd469c44e7f3e5a0cda6416964ec91d57de4ca06eb.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078661 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/q-14785.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y = f(x)$ above, find the value of\n$∫_{-3}^{4}f(x)dx$." 13194139534463 false] [17592186078661 1145 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1),$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius of $1,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078949 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534463 true] [17592186078949 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/69669250982a974c17cba2d70f27077f9c523ef184ff3f09650ea8d439428589.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078949 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/q-49212.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$, shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $∫_{-3}^{3}f(x)dx$." 13194139534463 false] [17592186078949 1145 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,0),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-0.5.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)(3) \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-0.5+4.5 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078972 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534463 true] [17592186078972 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of\n$∫_{-3}^{4}f(x)dx$?\n$\\displaystyle f(x)=\\begin{cases}4, & x<0, \\\\ -x+4, & x\\ge 0.\\end{cases}$" 13194139534463 false] [17592186078972 1100 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4, & x<0 \\\\ -x+4, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186078972 1145 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}4\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x{|}_{-3}^{0}+(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x){|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =[4(0)-4(-3)]+[-\\frac{(4)^{2}}{2}+4(4)+\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2}-4(0)] \\\\  & =[0+12]+[-8+16+0-0] \\\\  & =12+8 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289612 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289613 13194139534463 true] [17592186289612 1089 "q-117662" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289612 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534463 true] [17592186289612 1100 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x+5, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+13, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289612 1113 #uuid "8b6777ca-ff4c-593f-a186-002b5ace07e8" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289612 1145 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}(\\frac{1}{3}x+13)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+5x){|}_{-1}^{3}+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+13x){|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =[3\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+5(3)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-5(-1)]+[\\frac{{9}^{2}}{6}+13(9)-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{6}-13(3)] \\\\  & =[12+20]+[12+78] \\\\  & =32+90 \\\\  & =122.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289614 1