line segment for $x\\in (1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+5)4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)3 \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =12+6+7.5 \\\\  & =25.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289648 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289649 13194139534463 true] [17592186289648 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534463 true] [17592186289648 1041 "field-1" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289648 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289649 13194139534463 true] [17592186289648 1110 #uuid "a37dd813-ec61-5ea2-8e32-14acea737276" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289649 1034 "25.5" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289649 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534463 true] [17592186289649 1111 #uuid "9ef69d82-c2fa-59b5-97c1-d769b44d02e8" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289650 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289651 13194139534463 true] [17592186289650 1089 "q-338474" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289650 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534463 true] [17592186289650 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/44d4c8752c83db3b64b75b7b115902474856391ea87a21de1466a166816db929.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534463 true] [17592186289650 1113 #uuid "4cace43a-76f0-54d2-8e26-2c393b0daefe" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289650 1145 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the horizontal segment for $x\\in [-4,-1],$ the sloped segment for $x\\in (-1,0],$ and the sloped segment for $x\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle below the $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we subtract its area.\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289652 13194139534463 true] [17592186289651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289653 13194139534463 true] [17592186289651 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534463 true] [17592186289651 1041 "field-1" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289651 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289653 13194139534463 true] [17592186289651 1110 #uuid "72b15476-f1a9-5622-b285-fa765bfc2df6" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289652 1034 "11" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289652 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534463 true] [17592186289652 1111 #uuid "e475fc43-846c-5b63-af16-ceb7d14d5d17" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289653 1034 "10" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289653 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534463 true] [17592186289653 1111 #uuid "d5243a4d-dcd4-5ca5-b407-72cd6f66fa52" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289654 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289655 13194139534463 true] [17592186289654 1089 "q-339264" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289654 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534463 true] [17592186289654 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/db123092734fe2d84dacd2da2d005d001aadb673d1ad7f30c9527ef9f5a8ba7c.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534463 true] [17592186289654 1113 #uuid "e542677b-970f-5562-ac6a-54a112381d14" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289654 1145 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-1,3].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semicircle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+2\\pi  \\\\  & =8+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289655 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289656 13194139534463 true] [17592186289655 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534463 true] [17592186289655 1041 "field-1" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289655 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289656 13194139534463 true] [17592186289655 1110 #uuid "6ec77927-8866-56a5-a3b4-a52a1fa9c85d" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289656 1034 "8+2\\pi" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534463 true] [17592186289656 1111 #uuid "023fe5b1-a8b7-522e-b3f5-8df651e50105" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289657 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289658 13194139534463 true] [17592186289657 1089 "q-341981" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289657 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534463 true] [17592186289657 1100 "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5, & x<2, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534463 true] [17592186289657 1113 #uuid "a4321dc5-5a1f-5eae-acf4-e27f4a92f7a7" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289657 1145 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-5x){|}_{-4}^{2}\\,+\\,x{|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2}-5(2)\\,-\\,(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}-5(-4))]\\,+\\,[\\,6-2\\,] \\\\  & =[1-10\\,-\\,(4+20)]+4 \\\\  & =[-9-24]+4 \\\\  & =-33+4 \\\\  & =-29.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289659 13194139534463 true] [17592186289658 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534463 true] [17592186289658 1041 "field-1" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289658 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289659 13194139534463 true] [17592186289658 1110 #uuid "97084882-fc93-5592-8a8d-200228f92323" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289659 1034 "-29" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289659 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534463 true] [17592186289659 1111 #uuid "3cf407f2-5989-55ec-8135-061fe831e33d" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289660 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289661 13194139534463 true] [17592186289660 1089 "q-49209" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289660 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534463 true] [17592186289660 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/78500fa72c8f6aa49d95f34b88ce71ec79304203d7646200e98c311d8610200f.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289660 1113 #uuid "ed812b2f-487d-5fe1-bd24-af0e5cfc7e1f" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289660 1145 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,3).$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi {r}^{2})+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2})+4(2) \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,3).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi +8)+6 \\\\  & =2\\pi +14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289662 13194139534463 true] [17592186289661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289663 13194139534463 true] [17592186289661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289664 13194139534463 true] [17592186289661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289665 13194139534463 true] [17592186289661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289666 13194139534463 true] [17592186289661 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534463 true] [17592186289661 1041 "selection" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289661 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289666 13194139534463 true] [17592186289661 1110 #uuid "210868a4-a578-5362-9e93-f091d1cfdb9b" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289662 1034 "\\pi +16" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289662 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534463 true] [17592186289662 1111 #uuid "6e1c4e0a-a5f4-54b9-8263-b4a09d83cbe4" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289663 1034 "4\\pi +12" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289663 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534463 true] [17592186289663 1111 #uuid "3093ca09-ff01-5b89-a6df-52d1b6cabd3c" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289664 1034 "\\pi +11" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534463 true] [17592186289664 1111 #uuid "9b8fc3b0-7f3c-5b90-8142-b8a2d1469e7a" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289665 1034 "2\\pi +10" 13194139534463 true] [17592186289665 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534463 true] [17592186289665 1111 #uuid "5cc3a64b-5a64-51aa-9538-347ccbe3e387" 131941395344